 "result": "I couldn't get the answer. The database has returned \"database is locked. Try again.\" on every query, including a bare `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders`, across about 19 attempts. The lock doesn't look transient, so I stopped rather than keep going.\n\nI did confirm the schema, so the query is ready to run once the lock clears:\n\n- `orders(id, customer_id, item, total_eur, ordered_on)`\n- `customers(id, name, city)`\n- `ordered_on` is TEXT, so I'm assuming ISO dates (`YYYY-MM-DD`). I haven't checked that against real values.\n\nThe query I'd run is:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.ordered_on, o.customer_id, c.name\nFROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.ordered_on < '2026-10-01'\nORDER BY o.ordered_on DESC, o.id DESC\nLIMIT 1;\n```\n\nSomething else is probably holding a lock on the database file, such as another process or an open connection. Once that's released, I can rerun this. I can keep retrying in a new turn if you'd like.",
